+## Proposed Pack Conventions — Design for Review
+
+**Status: proposal, not an implementation announcement.** The APIs, named PAC
+headers, embedded offset directories, and fixed-width PAC mode in this section
+are being specified before refactoring. The existing libraries and wire format
+are documented below under [Current libraries](#current-libraries) and
+[Current format reference](#current-format-reference). Version zero remains
+under development; this document does not assign new wire identifiers or promise
+compatibility with proposed bytes that no implementation writes yet.
+
+PIL is **Positional Interchange Layout**. PAC is **Packed Array Container**.
+One Definition describes one PIL record. One PAC contains an ordered collection
+of PIL records bound to that exact Definition. A record can itself contain
+schema-defined arrays and child records. PAC member access addresses the outer
+PIL records; access inside a field follows that field's Definition.
+
+Pill Packer supplies layout encoding, packing, compression, and access primitives.
+The application chooses record shape, grouping, pack size, headers, validation
+policy, storage, and indexes. The same Definition can be used to pack any
+application-selected subset of its records. The library assigns no meaning to
+the contents or to application file names.
+
+### Definition and record preparation
+
+The following is language-neutral pseudocode. It proposes a shared contract
+across Rust, Go, and JavaScript/WASM, not currently callable package exports:
+
+```ts
+import * as pill from "pill-packer";
+
+const rxBytes = pill.rx.write(cddlJsonBytes);
+const cddlJsonAgain = pill.rx.read(rxBytes);
+const definition = pill.define(rxBytes); // prepare once, reuse
+
+const first = pill.fill(definition, [
+  field0Bytes,
+  field1Bytes,
+  pill.absent,
+  pill.null
+]);
+
+const pils = [first, externallyPreparedPilBytes]; // complete PIL byte sequences
+```
+
+- `rx.write` compiles supported CDDL schema JSON to a PIL-encoded `.rx` document.
+  `rx.read` renders that document's schema as JSON. CDDL source text remains an
+  authoring input supported by the existing CLI. JSON is outside the binary
+  record and PAC paths.
+- `define` retains the exact Definition bytes, digest, and reusable layout
+  information. Preparing a Definition is a separate cost from filling records.
+- `fill` accepts field payload bytes in Definition order. It writes the state
+  mask, integer selectors where applicable, DIRECT values, offsets, and variable
+  payloads. Explicit absent and null markers distinguish both states from present
+  empty bytes. It validates payload representation and schema constraints.
+- Encoded arrays and nested records follow their declared layouts. Generic
+  array/child-record helpers should be available through the same library.
+  A private byte block is opaque unless its structure is described in `.rx`.
+- Applications may prepare valid PIL bytes themselves and pass them directly to
+  `pack`. Calling `fill` is optional. Equivalent validation and record semantics
+  apply to either producer; performance measurements decide which is suitable.
+- A normal `fill` result owns its output bytes. A reusable-buffer variant is a
+  proposed optimization to measure separately; overwriting its buffer invalidates
+  any borrowed bytes. Borrowing and allocation must be explicit in each binding.
+
+The Definition governs the PIL representation. Choosing a PAC layout or codec
+does not change field meaning, synthesize absent values, or alter null semantics.
+
+### Packing API and application headers
+
+```ts
+const cpack = pill.pack(definition, pils, {
+  layout: { kind: "variable" },
+  packing: { commonValues: true },
+  compression: { algorithm: "gzip", level: compressionLevel },
+  headers: {
+    "Batch-Id": batchIdBytes,
+    "Producer": producerBytes
+  },
+  index: { addressBytes: 3 }
+});
+
+// The bytes can be stored or transmitted without a second serialization pass.
+const storedBytes = cpack.bytes;
+```
+
+Header names identify metadata fields; their values are opaque bytes. The object
+above represents ordinary named headers, such as `Batch-Id: <bytes>`. It is not a
+list of numeric extension descriptors. The library preserves custom header bytes
+and returns them when opening a pack. Their interpretation belongs to the caller.
+
+The proposed custom-header representation is a length-delimited collection of
+UTF-8 names and byte values. Names are nonempty and case-sensitive. Writers sort
+names by encoded bytes for deterministic output. Readers reject duplicate names,
+invalid lengths, invalid name encoding, and collisions with reserved structural
+names. Empty values are valid. Unknown custom headers are preserved. Applications
+enforce their own required custom-header names and value formats.
+
+Mandatory structural header fields describe the container:
+
+| Information | Supplied or computed |
+| --- | --- |
+| Exact Definition digest | Computed from the bound `.rx` bytes |
+| Record count and stored/decoded lengths | Computed from the completed pack |
+| Packing choices and compression codec/decoder parameters | Recorded from the requested options |
+| Member region boundaries and common-value metadata | Computed during packing |
+| Variable addressing mode and optional directory address width | Recorded from the requested options |
+| Fixed record width, when selected | Derived and verified, or checked against the supplied width |
+
+These fields have defined binary representations. Applications cannot overwrite
+them through custom headers. The exact byte offsets and discriminators for the
+proposed additions will be specified before codec changes.
+
+Minimal options mean variable members, no common-value factoring, no compression,
+no embedded index, and no custom headers. Full validation is the default.
+Applications explicitly enable the work and metadata they need.
+
+### Three addressing strategies
+
+All three strategies retain the same logical PIL values and record order.
+
+| Strategy | Requested options | Address metadata | Locating record `i` after opening |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Variable, sequential | `layout: {kind: "variable"}, index: false` | Count and member extent | Traverse preceding record boundaries |
+| Variable, indexed | `layout: {kind: "variable"}, index: {addressBytes: 2 \| 3 \| 4}` | Count plus packed starting offsets | Read adjacent directory entries |
+| Fixed width | `layout: {kind: "fixed"}, index: false` | Record width and count | `memberStart + i * recordWidth` |
+
+Compression is an independent choice for every strategy. There is no requirement
+to index variable records, or to turn a fixed-width pack into an indexed pack.
+
+#### Variable records with an optional embedded index
+
+The directory stores one unsigned little-endian starting offset per member.
+Offsets are relative to the beginning of the **decoded member region**, after
+packing and common-value factoring. They never refer to compressed byte positions
+or to the original input buffer.
+
+For `N` members and address width `w`, the directory costs `N * w` bytes in the
+decoded pack. The next starting offset gives the current record's end; the last
+record ends at the member region's declared end. No extra terminal entry is needed.
+
+| Address width | Bytes per member | Largest representable starting offset |
+| --- | ---: | ---: |
+| 2 | 2 | 65,535 |
+| 3 | 3 | 16,777,215 |
+| 4 | 4 | 4,294,967,295 |
+
+The width must represent every stored starting offset, subject also to the
+container's overall length limits. The final member end is supplied by the member
+extent, not another narrow directory entry. Choose width from the final decoded
+offsets, not record count or compressed size. Overflow is an error; a requested
+width is never silently widened or split into multiple packs.
+
+The first offset is zero. Remaining offsets must increase strictly and stay
+within the member region; each range must contain exactly one member. An empty
+pack has an empty directory. Readers check directory length against record count
+and width, and enforce their chosen validation and resource limits.
+
+Normally the packer generates this table while writing final members. Common
+factoring can change member sizes, so input-PIL offsets are not sufficient.
+A caller-supplied table may be accepted as:
+
+```ts
+index: { addressBytes: 3, offsets: packedOffsetBytes }
+```
+
+Its bytes must match the final member layout exactly. The writer can check that
+during its existing member pass. Supplying a table cannot bypass bounds or
+consistency checks. Validation cost must be reported separately from generation
+cost in benchmarks.
+
+Applications may continue to use external directories. They must bind an external
+directory to the exact pack representation and offset base. An embedded directory
+removes that separate binding requirement; it does not replace application lookup
+indexes that map keys to a pack and record number.
+
+#### Fixed width: record width and record count
+
+```ts
+const upack = pill.pack(definition, pils, {
+  layout: { kind: "fixed", recordWidth: expectedWidth },
+  packing: { commonValues: false },
+  compression: { algorithm: "none" },
+  headers,
+  index: false
+});
+```
+
+`recordWidth` is the size of each **final stored member**, including its ordinary
+PIL masks and any field offsets. It excludes the PAC header and common frame.
+The packer verifies equal member lengths after all selected packing operations.
+It also verifies `memberBytes == recordWidth * recordCount` with checked arithmetic.
+A supplied width is a constraint, never an instruction to pad records.
+
+For nonempty packs, the width may be omitted and derived from the first final
+member, then verified against every remaining member. An empty fixed-width pack
+requires an explicit positive width. Width metadata and count are sufficient for
+addressing, so the lighter fixed strategy omits the directory. A caller may
+explicitly request a redundant directory for a uniform consumer interface; its
+offsets must agree with the verified width and its extra cost is measured.
+
+Fixed scalar types alone do not guarantee a fixed record size: presence states,
+adaptive integers, arrays, and variable payloads can change lengths. Conversely,
+records containing variable fields may qualify when their actual final lengths
+are equal. The fixed mode verifies bytes; it does not infer a presence contract
+or reserve false positions for absent fields.
+
+A failed fixed-width request is an error. The library does not pad, fabricate
+values, truncate, or silently switch to variable mode. Common-value factoring
+remains available when explicitly selected, but its final members must still
+satisfy the requested width.
+
+### Common values, compression, and UPack/CPack
+
+Common-value factoring is a generic packing operation. It compares encoded field
+states and bytes at corresponding positions under the same Definition. Equal
+fields can be stored once and inherited by members. It neither aggregates
+records nor assigns meaning to fields. Codec-specific transforms, if added, must
+be explicit, reversible, and described by generic metadata; opaque application
+payloads are not privately transformed by the packer.
+
+`UPack` means an uncompressed PAC representation. `CPack` means a compressed
+representation that expands to a UPack. These are representation names and handle
+types, not required filename extensions or different logical record collections.
+
+The proposed compressed representation wraps the complete UPack, following the
+current whole-container compression model. Its small outer control header records
+the codec and necessary framing/decoded-size controls. Named headers, common data,
+and any offset directory are inside the decoded UPack and become available on
+unpack. Compression cost and compression of directory/header metadata must be
+measured explicitly.
+
+The caller chooses compression through the pack request. Initially `none` and
+`gzip` are the implemented-codec targets for the proposed API. A generic codec
+registration mechanism should allow other algorithms without application-specific
+branches: a stable codec identifier, encoder, bounded decoder, and any required
+decoder parameters. Registering a codec is explicit; unknown codecs are rejected.
+
+A requested codec is honored even if it expands the input. Falling back to
+uncompressed output is allowed only through an explicit caller policy such as
+`onlyIfSmaller: true`. The returned handle and serialized discriminator identify
+the representation actually produced. Compression effort is selected by the
+caller; a codec has no hardcoded workload-specific level or threshold.
+
+### Opening and retrieving records
+
+```ts
+const { pack: upack, headers } = pill.unpack(definition, cpack.bytes);
+
+const record = upack.pop(17);                    // zero-based record number
+const sameRecord = upack.pop({ index: 17 });      // explicit equivalent
+const byAddress = upack.pop({ offset: offset }); // decoded member-relative start
+```
+
+`unpack` accepts compressed or uncompressed PAC bytes, verifies the Definition
+binding and framing, and returns an opened UPack plus its named headers. The
+compression discriminator selects the registered decoder; callers need not repeat
+the packing algorithm to decode a self-describing pack. Allowed codecs, limits,
+and validation policy can be supplied separately as reader options.
+
+An opened UPack retains its Definition, common data, member extent, and addressing
+information. It holds any reusable traversal state. Packing an uncompressed result
+can return an already opened handle, avoiding a serialize-and-rescan cycle.
+
+`pop` is the proposed extraction name: it returns a complete standalone PIL and
+does **not** remove or renumber records. An integer always means record number;
+byte-offset access is explicit. Offsets must identify actual member starts.
+For fixed members, alignment checks locate valid starts. With a directory, lookup
+can establish membership. Without one, verifying a supplied offset may require
+traversal. A position inside a member is invalid.
+
+Common fields must be restored when returning a standalone PIL. Locating a member
+can be constant time while reconstruction/copying still costs time proportional
+to the returned record size. A borrowed view API may expose common/member spans
+for readers that do not need standalone bytes; it must document lifetime and
+mutability instead of hiding copies.
+
+Full validation may scan all members when opening even an indexed or fixed pack.
+Trusted reception can use explicitly selected structural checks after establishing
+producer validation and integrity. Opening cost, validation cost, and subsequent
+member lookup cost must be measured separately.
+
+Opening an unindexed pack must not silently allocate a complete offset table.
+Validation can scan without retaining every boundary. Any auxiliary in-memory
+index is an explicit opt-in with its own allocation and construction costs.
+Fixed-width addressing likewise needs no allocation proportional to record count.
+
+Whole-pack compression requires expanding the pack before direct member access.
+An offset directory or fixed stride does not enable seeking within a gzip stream.
+`pop` on an opened UPack must not repeatedly decompress the original bytes.
+Independently compressed blocks would be a separate optional strategy requiring
+its own access, size, and CPU evaluation.
+
+Application indexes can store `(pack identity, record number)` instead of a full
+member byte address. Their record-number width depends on member count. It is
+independent of the PAC directory's byte-offset width. For example, 50,000 records
+fit two-byte ordinals while their member region may need three-byte offsets.
+
+### Baseline and cost evaluation before refactoring
+
+**No results for the proposed modes are claimed here.** Capture the current
+implementation baseline before changing behavior. Record the revision and exact
+working-tree source snapshot, toolchain versions, platform, build flags, fixtures,
+and deterministic input seeds. Existing Rust codec/reference benchmarks are
+starting points, not evidence that the proposed pack modes already exist.
+
+Build a generic benchmark harness in this repository. Application-specific
+integration measurements and data generators belong in their consuming projects.
+
+| Dimension | Cases |
+| --- | --- |
+| Producer | Existing external PIL builder; library `fill`; reused-output-buffer variant |
+| Record shape | Dense equal-width records; sparse records; variable strings/bytes; arrays and nested values |
+| Addressing | Variable scan; variable index at 2/3/4 bytes when representable; verified fixed width |
+| Packing | Common values disabled/enabled; low/high repetition; mixed presence states |
+| Compression | None; gzip-1; gzip-6; additional registered codecs only when implemented |
+| Read workload | Whole-pack traversal; first/middle/last lookup; seeded random lookups; repeated access to an opened pack |
+| Reader policy | Full validation; eligible structural/trusted reception, reported separately |
+| Pack size | Empty/singleton, small batches, tens of thousands, and large batches up to configured limits |
+
+Use identical logical records, ordering, headers, grouping, and codec effort for
+each valid comparison. Fixed-width comparisons use records that actually qualify;
+they must not introduce dummy values or drop fields to make a fixture fit.
+Inapplicable narrow-index cases are reported as inapplicable. Address-width
+boundary fixtures exercise offsets around 65,535/65,536 and
+16,777,215/16,777,216. Additional checks cover empty packs, arithmetic limits,
+malformed directories, absent/null/empty values, and exact integer preservation.
+
+Measure and report:
+
+- Definition preparation and schema compilation separately from record operations.
+- Fill, pack, and optional directory generation/validation time, including
+  allocations and allocated bytes per record or pack.
+- Header, common-data, directory, member, and total byte sizes before and after
+  compression. Indexed directory overhead starts at `recordCount * addressBytes`;
+  fixed width has constant width/count metadata and no member directory.
+- Decompression, opening/validation, traversal, index construction for an
+  unindexed opened pack if offered, lookup, and standalone-record reconstruction.
+- Throughput, repeated-sample latency distributions, retained memory, and peak
+  memory. Include data copies and temporary buffers in the cost.
+- Cold opening versus repeated lookups on one already opened pack. Separate
+  in-memory codec measurements from storage I/O, FFI/WASM crossings, and rendering.
+- Optional sidecar versus embedded directories, and short application ordinals
+  versus full byte addresses, in integration reports.
+
+Use optimized builds, warm-up where applicable, repeated runs, and retained raw
+results. Report measurement variability and any runtime/OS cache limitations.
+Exercise Rust, Go, and JavaScript/WASM for representation agreement and identify
+which binding each timing measures. Existing baseline commands include:
+
+```sh
+cargo bench --locked --manifest-path rust/Cargo.toml --bench codecs
+cargo bench --locked --manifest-path rust/Cargo.toml --bench shared_refs
+```
+
+The proposed PAC strategy benchmark harness is additional work; no new benchmark
+command is presented as runnable yet. Baseline capture must precede refactoring,
+and each implementation step should include targeted correctness checks and a
+comparable cost report. Avoid using historical payload-size examples below as
+performance evidence for a new addressing or compression strategy.
+
+### Proposed implementation sequence
+
+1. Review this API contract and specify the binary header, layout descriptors,
+   named-header encoding, directory placement, and codec identifiers.
+2. Capture a reproducible baseline of current behavior and build the comparison
+   fixtures without changing their record semantics.
+3. Implement the generic fill/pack/open/extract contract and optional addressing
+   modes in the existing Rust and Go codec layers. Expose matching FFI and
+   JavaScript/WASM APIs; reuse existing encoders, validators, and scanners.
+4. Check cross-binding round trips, header preservation, common-field restoration,
+   fixed-width verification, directory bounds, and explicit codec policies.
+5. Evaluate each strategy against the baseline before applications adopt it.
+   Applications then choose their own grouping, record construction, header
+   values, and indexing/compression policy.
+
+Named PAC headers, embedded indexes, and fixed-width PACs are all proposals in this
+section. Current numeric extensions, external directory helpers, and current
+compression behavior are described below as existing behavior, not as the agreed
+implementation of this proposed API.
